'''''Pyracantha crenulata''''', the '''Nepalese firethorn''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 |df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref>{{PLANTS|id=PYCR7|taxon=Pyracantha crenulata |access-date=16 October 2015}}</ref><ref name=GRIN>{{GRIN | access-date=2010-12-22}}</ref> '''Nepal firethorn''' or '''Himalayan firethorn''', is a species of [[Pyracantha|firethorn]]. The native range of this shrub species stretches from northern Pakistan to northwestern India and China.

==Description==
''Pyracantha crenulata'' is a [[deciduous]], bushy and profusely branched and very thorny shrub. It grows up to {{cvt|2.4|–|3.0|m|1}} tall. It has dark brown [[stem bark]] which becomes [[glabrous]] (hairless) when mature. It has dark green leaves,<ref name=Singh/> with a smooth exterior. They are {{cvt|2.5|–|4.0|cm|1}} long and {{cvt|1.0|–|2.2|cm|1}} wide with a tapering end.<ref name="Joshi2023"/>
Flowering takes place between April and May.<ref name=Singh/> The flowers are [[Hermaphrodite (botany)|hermaphrodite]] (bisexual),<ref name="Joshi2023"/><ref name=Singh/> the white inflorescence is a compound [[corymb]] composed of many flowers. The flowers have 20 [[stamens]] and one [[Ovary (botany)|ovary]] in the centre.<ref name=Singh/> Every flower has five sepals and five petals.<ref name="Joshi2023"/> The fruits ripen between June and September, with orange-red to dark red berry-like fruits.<ref name=Singh/> The berries are small, and each berry weighs about 250&nbsp;mg.<ref name="Joshi2023"/> Each berry generally contains five triangular shaped and brown-colored seeds. There are sometimes three or four seeds per berry and the seeds are covered with a hard seed coat.<ref name="Joshi2023"/>

==Taxonomy==
It is locally named as Ghingharu (घिंगारू) in [[Kumaon region]] of [[Uttarakhand]], India.<ref name=Singh/>

It was first published in Fam. Nat. Syn. Monogr. 3: 220 in 1847.<ref name="Powo"/>

==Distribution==
It is a native species to the countries (and regions) of; Assam, China, East and West Himalayas, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Pakistan, Tibet and Vietnam.<ref name="Powo"/>

It has been introduced into: United States (within the states of Alabama, California, Florida, Georgia, Louisiana, North Carolina, Oregon, South Carolina, Texas and Washington), South America (in north-eastern Argentina and [[Guatemala]]), Europe (Spain), South Africa (within [[Free State (province)|Free State]], [[Lesotho]] and [[Northern Provinces]]), Australia (in [[New South Wales]]) and north and south New Zealand.<ref name="Powo"/>

==Habitat==
''Pyracantha crenulata'' is found growing in barren, rocky, and dry grasslands.<ref name=Singh/> It is also found along streams on the bank of streams and tributaries, in shrubberies, on open slopes, in cultivated areas and along roadsides.<ref name="Joshi2023">{{cite journal |last1=Joshi |first1=Rakesh Kumar |last2=Laurindo |first2=Lucas Fornari |last3=Barbalho |first3=Sandra M. |title=Chemical components and bioactivities of Pyracantha crenulata (D. Don) wild and cultivated from Uttarakhand, India: A review |journal=American Journal of Essential Oils and Natural Products |date=2023 |volume=11 |issue=1 |pages=34–37}}</ref>

On the Himalayan hills of [[Uttarakhand]], it grows in areas at an altitude of {{convert|900|-|1200|m|ft|abbr=on}} above sea level,<ref name=Singh/> within [[Pine]] and [[Quercus]] forests (Osmastan 1926).<ref>{{cite book |last1=Osmaston |first1=A.E. |title=A forest flora for Kumaon. |date=1926 |publisher=Periodical Experts Book Agency |location=Delhi}}</ref>

==Uses==
It is cultivated as an [[ornamental plant]]. The leaves are used to make herbal tea. The wood can be used to make walking sticks. The [[pome]] fruit is orange-red and are food for birds.

The plant is used in the Himalayas within herbal drugs and is usually collected from forest areas for therapeutic arrangements, such as brews of dehydrated fruits, fluid sources, and making [[tinctures]]. ''P. crenulate'' has been shown to help in the treatment of heart failure, cardiac issues, and [[hypertension]]. When eaten with yogurt, the berries of the shrub help patients recover from [[dysentery]] (Singh et al. 2012).<ref name=Singh>{{cite book |last1=Singh |first1=R. |last2=Negi |first2=P.S. |last3=Dwivedi |first3=S.K. |title=New age herbals |date=2018 |publisher=Springer. |pages=135–149 |chapter=Indian Hawthorn (''Pyracantha crenulata'').}}</ref>

It is also used for cultivation (in the Himalayas) near the boundaries of farming areas to stop soil erosion happening during the [[monsoon]] season due to its good soil binder capacity (via the roots) and it is used to prevent the entrance of wild animals (thorny bushes) into farming fields of vegetables and cereals.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Gamble |first1=J.S. |title=A manual of  Indian timbers: an  account of the growth, distribution, and uses of the trees and shrubs of India and Ceylon, with descriptions of their wood-structure. |date=1972 |publisher=Bishen Singh Mahindra Pal Singh Publication |location=Dehradun}}</ref>

